WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.119
Secure transport is the foundation for modern web security.

2
00:00:04.119 --> 00:00:12.114
Before a browser can enforce meaningful page behavior, the connection itself needs confidentiality and integrity.

3
00:00:12.114 --> 00:00:19.462
HTTPS protects requests and responses in transit and helps users know they are talking to the intended site.

4
00:00:19.462 --> 00:00:28.588
Without it, credentials, session cookies, form data, and page content can be exposed or modified between the browser and the service.

5
00:00:28.588 --> 00:00:36.260
Strict-Transport-Security, usually called HSTS, tells browsers to use HTTPS for future connections to a site.

6
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.025
The max-age directive tells the browser how long to remember the rule.

7
00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:44.578
includeSubDomains extends the rule to subdomains.

8
00:00:44.578 --> 00:00:52.088
Preload is an additional ecosystem-level commitment where a domain may be included in browser preload lists.

9
00:00:52.088 --> 00:00:59.437
These options are powerful because they reduce opportunities for downgrade and mixed transport mistakes.

10
00:00:59.437 --> 00:01:01.133
HSTS rollout needs care.

11
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:13.408
If a domain uses includeSubDomains or preload before every subdomain and legacy service is ready for HTTPS, legitimate users can be locked out of services that still depend on HTTP.

12
00:01:13.408 --> 00:01:15.831
The fix is not to avoid HSTS forever.

13
00:01:15.831 --> 00:01:29.641
The fix is to inventory subdomains, validate certificates and redirects, stage the rollout, use appropriate max-age values during testing, and increase commitment only when the environment is ready.

14
00:01:29.641 --> 00:01:35.294
HTTPS redirection and mixed content handling are part of the same transport story.

15
00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:46.115
The server should redirect HTTP requests to HTTPS, and the application should avoid loading insecure scripts, images, frames, or API calls from secure pages.

16
00:01:46.115 --> 00:02:00.571
Transport headers reduce risk when they are paired with platform hygiene: valid certificates, consistent redirects, secure cookies, and deployment processes that do not accidentally reintroduce HTTP paths.
